  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nShowers and storms tonight...Warm front is lifting north of \nWisconsin. Surface instability is limited to southern Minnesota \non nose of 55-60 surface dewpoints. Surface instability remains \nwest of central Wisconsin into the evening, even as low-level jet \nup 45+ kts arrives ahead of cold front tied to surface low lifting\ninto western Lake Superior to western Ontario Canada. Showers and\nthunderstorms will develop on nose of the LLJ and lift into \ncentral and north-central Wisconsin. Even eastern areas will see\nmeasurable rain, though bulk of QPF (around 0.5 inch) will occur\nover north-central Wisconsin. Given strength of winds aloft  \n(effective shear up to 35 kts) and elevated instability (500+ \nj/kg), cannot rule out an isolated 50+ mph gust if a line segment \ncan get going perpendicular to the strong winds aloft, but minimal\nsurface based instability due to low-level inversion and later \narrival of storms past dark (peak coverage 7 pm to 11 pm), along \nwith lack of strong forcing and weaker lapse rates seem to point \nto weakening convection as it moves in. \n\nWe are in a favored portion of larger scale trough for Gravity \nWave Associated Convection (GWAC), but various model reflectivity\nfields don't show the usual look of separated pulses of higher \ncores which you sometimes see with GWAC and HREF/REFS data has a \nnon-existent signal for any higher peak wind gusts. Overall this \nsetup has a look of brief downpours, gusty winds to 40 mph with \nthe stronger storms, especially over north-central Wisconsin. SPC \nDay1 outlook does clip our north-central, but at this point it \nseems like a pretty low-end marginal risk for our area. Rain ends\nlate tonight into early Thursday as the cold front shifts through.\n\nDry late this week, then rain chances this weekend...High pressure\nsettles overhead late Thursday into Friday. Main impact could be\nsome fog on Thursday night. Coolest temperatures for the next week\nwill be Thursday night into Friday, but even during the coolest day\nreadings will still be above normal. Southern stream wave lifts\ntoward the state on Saturday, but with limited surface reflection\nassociated rain showers with it will diminish as it moves farther\nnorth. Greatest chances for rain (20-30%) will be over central and\neast-central Wisconsin. Thus, could see scattered showers for any\nHalloween activities, but not expecting a washout. \n\nLooking ahead, small chances of rain could linger Sunday-Monday, \nbut better chances continue to ramp up (up to 60%) for Tuesday \nwhich is Election Day. May also see gusty east winds and cooler \ntemps than current forecast shows especially if stronger surface \nlow exits the plains to the midwest/Ohio Valley as has occasionally\nbeen shown by models last few days. NBM/LREF probabilities for at\nleast 0.05 inch of rain on Tuesday continue to be higher.",
